#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1320.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092973} :activity/id #uuid "81af9a68-b130-5677-9448-07c433454ec2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048625 :topic/id #uuid "af9f4926-ffe7-585c-be25-f4e24467b430" :topic/math-academy-id 921} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092973 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093391 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find all of the solutions to\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\dfrac{1}{2}, \n\t\\qquad -\\pi \\leq x < \\pi.\n$$\n\nNotice that the equation contains a transformed cosine function. \n\nWe know that the general solution to the elementary equation $\\cos{X} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & X & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is assumed to be any integer.\n\nTo find the general solution of the transformed cosine equation, we replace $X$ with the input of the cosine function, which is $x + \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ in this case. This gives the general solution \n\n$$\nx+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + 2n\\pi, \\qquad\\qquad x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}+ 2n\\pi.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above two equations for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi  \\\\  &  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+\\frac{6n\\pi }{3} \\\\  &  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{2\\pi (2+3n)}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $n = 0, \\pm1, \\pm2, \\ldots$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi \\leq x < \\pi.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=0$ and $x=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}.$ We reject the solution $\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\n- Setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-2\\pi$ and $x=-\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.$ We reject the solution $-2\\pi$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi \\leq x < \\pi.$ So, the solutions $x_1$ and $x_2$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx_1 = -\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},\\qquad x_2 = 0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ca38e435-6d4e-550b-83d2-62b4e80d4eda" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2684 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "724852af-67e5-54bd-91c0-766dd3dabaf7" :step/math-academy-id 13025 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092974}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092974 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092172 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091991 :question/id #uuid "833de87d-9983-5f8d-94aa-3132606fc15d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3775"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6d31a25-823d-59b2-897c-61a75b747c01" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049936}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543556}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Containing the Cosine Function Transformed by One Operation"} :step/id #uuid "23e0ef78-2c4c-5cea-9f7b-95ad132ae0f0" :step/math-academy-id 13039 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092975}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092975 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092173 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091993 :question/id #uuid "d8397378-838b-5fb1-b3e7-b5c341a17e38" :question/math-academy-id "e-3784"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1286cc9b-2080-5541-88e1-d4d2741ea1e5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543558}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Containing the Cosine Function Transformed by Multiple Operations"} :step/id #uuid "e4478e20-2cd6-5665-be7e-0489a9f6197c" :step/math-academy-id 13046 :s